export const H1={title:'The phlebotomist, the event and professional practice',hook:'Laboratory results are often said to inform about 70% of medical decisions, and nearly every blood test starts with a phlebotomist. A mislabeled tube, the wrong additive or a draw from the wrong patient can lead to a wrong diagnosis or a dangerous transfusion. This lesson introduces the profession, the HOSA event and the communication, clerical, legal and time-management skills that make up nearly a quarter of the test.',objectives:['Describe the Phlebotomy event, its test plan and its nine skills.','Explain the phlebotomist’s role, settings and credentials.','Identify patients correctly and process requisitions accurately.','Apply consent, confidentiality, scope-of-practice and time-management principles.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'Phlebotomy has two rounds. **Round One** is a **50-question test in 60 minutes**: obtaining blood samples, including which tubes are used for specific tests (**42%**); specimen collection and processing (**16%**); safety standards and procedures (**10%**); time management and assignment organization (**8%**); legal, ethical and professional considerations (**8%**); terminology, anatomy and physiology (**8%**); professional communications (**4%**); and clerical skills (**4%**). The references are Booth and Mundt’s *Phlebotomy: A Competency Based Approach* and McCall’s *Phlebotomy Essentials*.',
'Top scorers advance to **Round Two**, which may include any of these skills: **I** handwashing (4 minutes), **II** tourniquet application (2), **III-A** routine ETS venipuncture with order of draw (10), **III-B** butterfly venipuncture with order of draw (10), **IV** a patient who starts to faint (5), **V** heel puncture (5), **VI** removing contaminated gloves (3), **VII** accidental arterial stick (5) and **VIII** 24-hour urine collection (5). Scenarios can combine skills; you need **70%** on the combined skills to be recognized, and ties go to the higher test score. Bring photo ID and at least **two pairs of non-latex gloves**.',
{t:'table',caption:'Written test plan',head:['Area','Weight'],rows:[['Obtaining blood samples (including tubes for tests)','42%'],['Specimen collection and processing','16%'],['Safety standards and procedures','10%'],['Time management and organization','8%'],['Legal, ethical and professional','8%'],['Terminology, anatomy and physiology','8%'],['Professional communications','4%'],['Clerical skills and duties','4%']]}]],
['The phlebotomist’s role',[
'A **phlebotomist** collects blood (and some other specimens) for laboratory testing, transfusion or donation. Phlebotomists work in **hospitals**, **outpatient laboratories**, **physician offices**, **blood donor centers**, **nursing homes** and as **mobile** collectors. Duties include identifying patients, choosing equipment, performing venipunctures and capillary punctures, labeling and transporting specimens, processing samples, performing some **point-of-care tests** and keeping records.',
'Most employers prefer **national certification**, such as AMT’s **Registered Phlebotomy Technician (RPT)**, NHA’s CPT or ASCP’s PBT, and a few states (including California) require a state license. The field is growing as the population ages. Laboratories that test human specimens are regulated by **CLIA**, which sets quality standards by test complexity.',
sort('Phlebotomist duty or not?',['Phlebotomist duty','Outside phlebotomist scope'],[['Identifying the patient with two identifiers',0],['Labeling tubes at the bedside',0],['Interpreting test results for the patient',1],['Centrifuging and aliquoting serum',0],['Ordering tests the doctor did not request',1],['Prescribing medicine for a fainting patient',1]])]],
['Patient identification and requisitions',[
'Every collection begins with a **requisition**—a paper or electronic order listing the patient’s name, date of birth, ID number, ordering provider, tests, collection priority (such as **STAT**, **ASAP**, **timed** or **routine**) and special requirements such as fasting. In Skill III you review the requisition, greet the patient and introduce yourself, ask the patient to **state their full name and date of birth and spell their first and last names**, and **verbally match** these to the requisition. The Joint Commission requires **at least two identifiers**; a room number is never an identifier.',
'Inpatients also wear an **ID band**, which must match the requisition exactly. If there is any mismatch, missing band or confusion, **do not draw**—notify the nurse and resolve it first. Unconscious patients, newborns and patients who do not speak your language need special identification procedures and interpreters. Wrong-patient errors can be fatal in blood banking, so many hospitals use barcode scanning at the bedside.',
order('Identify before you draw',['Review the requisition','Greet the patient and introduce yourself','Ask the patient to state and spell full name and give date of birth','Compare with the requisition (and ID band for inpatients)','Resolve any mismatch before collection','Explain the procedure and obtain consent'])]],
['Consent, privacy and the law',[
'Patients must **consent** before collection. Most venipunctures rely on **implied consent** (the patient extends an arm after you explain the procedure), but some tests—such as HIV testing in certain states—require written **informed consent**. A competent adult may **refuse**; respect the refusal, do not force or threaten, and notify the nurse or provider. Drawing blood without consent can be **assault and battery**, and incorrect technique causing injury can be **negligence** or **malpractice**.',
'Under **HIPAA**, patient information is shared only with people who need it for care. Specimens and requisitions are kept out of public view, and results are never discussed with friends or family without authorization. Phlebotomists also follow **scope of practice**: they do not interpret results, diagnose or give medical advice. Professional conduct includes punctuality, a neat appearance, honesty about errors and respect for every patient’s culture and dignity.',
match('Legal terms',[['Implied consent','Patient’s actions show agreement, such as extending an arm'],['Informed consent','Patient is told the risks and agrees, often in writing'],['Assault','A threat or attempt to touch someone without consent'],['Battery','Touching someone without consent'],['Negligence','Failure to act as a reasonable professional would'],['HIPAA','Federal law protecting health information']])]],
['Communication and time management',[
'Good communication calms anxious patients and prevents errors. Introduce yourself, use plain language, explain what the patient will feel, warn them before the needlestick, listen to concerns (“I faint when I see blood”) and use open body language. With children, speak to them at eye level and involve parents; with older adults, speak clearly and allow extra time; use interpreters rather than family members when possible. Always thank the patient.',
'Phlebotomists manage many requests at once, so **prioritize**: **STAT** orders (critical, immediately) first, then **timed** draws (such as peak and trough drug levels or glucose tolerance tests), then **ASAP** and **routine** orders. Morning rounds collect fasting samples early. Organize your tray before entering rooms, check for special handling needs (ice, light protection) in advance and communicate delays to the nurse.',
sort('Draw first or later?',['Top priority','Lower priority'],[['STAT troponin from the emergency department',0],['Routine annual cholesterol',1],['Timed trough level due in 10 minutes',0],['Routine CBC for a stable patient',1],['STAT blood culture for a patient with fever and low blood pressure',0]])]],
['Practice: starting right',[
kase('Identification problem',{a:{text:'An inpatient’s requisition says “Maria Lopez, DOB 3/14/1960.” The patient states “Maria Lopes, March 14, 1960,” and her ID band reads “Maria Lopes.” What do you do? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['Do not draw; notify the nurse to resolve the mismatch','b','Correct. Every identifier must match exactly.',true],['Draw anyway—it is probably a typo','b','Never draw with mismatched identification.',false]]},b:{text:'The nurse corrects the requisition. The patient now says, “I don’t want my blood drawn today.” What next?',choices:[['Respect the refusal and notify the nurse','c','Correct. Competent adults may refuse.',true],['Draw quickly before she changes her mind','c','That would be battery.',false]]},c:{text:'The nurse talks with the patient, who later agrees after her questions are answered. You re-identify her before drawing.',end:true}}),
{t:'callout',kind:'exam',title:'Rating sheet habit',text:'Almost every Round Two skill gives points for appropriate verbal and nonverbal communication and for practicing standard precautions throughout. Say steps out loud when equipment is simulated, and thank the patient at the end.'}]]
],takeaways:['Round One: 50 questions in 60 minutes, with obtaining blood samples 42%; Round Two draws on nine skills and needs 70% overall.','Identify every patient with at least two identifiers (state and spell name, DOB) matched to the requisition and ID band; never draw on a mismatch.','Obtain consent, respect refusals, protect privacy under HIPAA, stay in scope and prioritize STAT, then timed, then routine draws.'],terms:[['Requisition','The order form listing the patient, tests and collection requirements.'],['STAT','A test that must be collected and processed immediately.'],['Implied consent','Agreement shown by the patient’s actions.']],mcqs:[q('What percentage of the Phlebotomy test is about obtaining blood samples?','42%','16%','10%','8%','The largest area.'),q('How many patient identifiers are required?','At least two','One','Three including room number','None for outpatients','Room numbers are not identifiers.'),q('How do you identify the patient in Skill III?','Ask the patient to state and spell their full name and give their date of birth','Read the name to the patient and ask “Is that you?”','Check the room number','Ask the nurse','Patients must state their information.'),q('What should you do if the ID band and requisition do not match?','Do not draw; notify the nurse','Draw and fix it later','Use the requisition','Use the band','Resolve the mismatch first.'),q('Drawing blood from a patient who refused is:','Battery','Implied consent','Negligence only','Acceptable for STAT orders','Touching without consent.'),q('Which order should be collected first?','STAT','Routine','ASAP','Fasting the next day','STAT means immediately.'),q('Which credential does AMT award phlebotomists?','RPT','CPhT','RN','CMA','Registered Phlebotomy Technician.'),q('Which federal law regulates laboratory testing quality?','CLIA','HIPAA','OSHA','EMTALA','Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendments.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how a phlebotomist begins a routine collection—from receiving the requisition to the moment before the tourniquet is applied—including identification, consent, communication and prioritization.',rubric:[['Requisition and priority',['requisition','stat','timed','routine','fasting','special']],['Identification',['two identifiers','state','spell','date of birth','match','band','mismatch']],['Consent and law',['explain','consent','refuse','nurse','hipaa','privacy']],['Communication',['introduce','plain','listen','warn','faint','thank']]],model:'I review the requisition for the patient’s name, date of birth, ID number, ordering provider, tests and priority, and I organize my work so STAT orders come first, then timed collections, then ASAP and routine orders. I check for special requirements such as fasting or chilling and prepare my tray. At the patient, I greet them, introduce myself and ask them to state their full name and date of birth and spell their first and last names; I verbally match these to the requisition and, for inpatients, to the ID band. If anything does not match, I do not draw and I notify the nurse. I explain the procedure in plain language and obtain consent—usually implied when the patient extends an arm—and if a competent patient refuses, I respect it and notify the nurse. I keep information private under HIPAA, listen for concerns such as a history of fainting so I can have the patient lie down, verify collection requirements and sensitivities such as latex allergy and then perform hand hygiene and put on gloves before positioning the arm.'},sources:[RULES,AMT,BLS_PHL,NPSG,CLIA]};

export const H2={title:'Safety, infection control and gloves (Skills I and VI)',hook:'Phlebotomists handle sharp needles and human blood dozens of times a day. Before safety-engineered needles and federal rules, healthcare workers suffered hundreds of thousands of needlestick injuries every year, some leading to hepatitis B, hepatitis C or HIV infections. Today the rules are clear—and two of them, handwashing and glove removal, are Round Two skills.',objectives:['Explain the chain of infection, standard precautions and the OSHA Bloodborne Pathogens Standard.','Wash hands following HOSA Skill I, including the correct times.','Remove contaminated gloves following HOSA Skill VI.','Respond correctly to a needlestick or exposure and follow other laboratory safety rules.'],sections:[
['Infection control basics',[
'Infections spread through the **chain of infection**: an **infectious agent**, a **reservoir**, a **portal of exit**, a **mode of transmission**, a **portal of entry** and a **susceptible host**. Breaking any link stops the spread. **Standard precautions** treat **all blood and body fluids as potentially infectious**: hand hygiene, gloves and other PPE as needed, safe sharps handling, respiratory etiquette and cleaning surfaces with an approved disinfectant (such as a 1:10 bleach dilution made fresh).',
'**Transmission-based precautions** add protection for known infections: **contact** (gown and gloves), **droplet** (mask) and **airborne** (N95 respirator, negative-pressure room). Some patients need **protective (reverse) isolation** because their immune systems are weak. Read isolation signs before entering, gather supplies first and take only what you need into the room.',
order('Chain of infection',['Infectious agent','Reservoir','Portal of exit','Mode of transmission','Portal of entry','Susceptible host'])]],
['Handwashing: Skill I',[
'Skill I has 14 steps in 4 minutes. **1.** Remove all jewelry. **2.** Turn on the water and adjust it to **warm**. **3.** Wet hands liberally with **fingertips pointing down**. **4.** Never let clothing touch the sink or touch the inside of the sink with your hands. **5.** Apply soap and work up a lather using a **circular motion with friction**. **6.** **Interlace fingers** and rub vigorously. **7–8.** **Scratch the nails** of each hand against the opposite palm. **9.** Thoroughly wash the **wrists**.',
'**10.** Verbalize the washing time: **2 minutes at the start of the workday**, **20 seconds between patients and procedures**, or **1–2 minutes if hands are soiled** (the scenario will tell you which). **11.** Rinse with hands pointed down and **lower than the elbows**. **12.** Dry hands and wrists with a **patting motion** using a clean paper towel. **13.** Discard the towel without touching the receptacle. **14.** If the sink is not foot-operated, use a **clean, dry towel to turn off the faucet**.',
match('Scenario → washing time',[['Start of the workday','2 minutes'],['Between patients','20 seconds'],['Hands visibly soiled','1–2 minutes'],['Rinse position','Hands down, lower than the elbows'],['Drying','Pat with a clean paper towel'],['Faucet off','Clean, dry paper towel']])]],
['Gloves: Skill VI',[
'Gloves protect you from blood but can carry germs to the next surface if removed wrongly. Skill VI has 8 steps in 3 minutes. **1.** Grasp the **outside of one glove below the wrist** with the opposite gloved hand. **2.** Pull it **inside out** over the hand. **3.** Pull it off and **hold it in the gloved hand**. **4.** Slip the **fingers of the bare hand under the wrist** of the remaining glove.',
'**5.** Pull that glove inside out over the hand so the first glove ends up **inside the second**, with **no exterior surfaces exposed**. **6.** Place the gloves in the proper waste receptacle. **7.** Perform hand hygiene. **8.** No contact is made with contaminated surfaces throughout. Change gloves between patients, when torn and when visibly soiled; never wash or reuse disposable gloves. Use **non-latex** gloves to protect people with latex allergy.',
order('Removing contaminated gloves',['Grasp the outside of one glove below the wrist','Pull it inside out and off','Hold it in the gloved hand','Slip bare fingers under the other glove’s wrist','Pull inside out over the first glove','Discard and perform hand hygiene'])]],
['Sharps safety and exposure response',[
'The **OSHA Bloodborne Pathogens Standard** requires employers to have an **exposure control plan**, provide PPE and training, offer the **hepatitis B vaccine** free to at-risk workers and use **engineering controls**. The **Needlestick Safety and Prevention Act (2000)** requires **safety-engineered sharps** and involving workers in choosing them. **Never recap** a used needle by hand, **activate the safety feature immediately** after the needle leaves the vein and discard the whole collection unit in a **puncture-resistant sharps container** that is replaced when about **three-quarters full**.',
'If you are exposed—a needlestick or blood splash to the eyes, nose, mouth or broken skin—**wash the area with soap and water** (flush eyes or mucous membranes with water), **report immediately** to your supervisor, document the incident and get **medical evaluation promptly**. Post-exposure prophylaxis for HIV works best when started within hours. The source patient may be tested with consent.',
sort('Safe or unsafe?',['Safe practice','Unsafe practice'],[['Activating the needle safety device right after withdrawal',0],['Recapping a used needle with two hands',1],['Replacing a sharps container when three-quarters full',0],['Pushing sharps down into a full container',1],['Reporting a needlestick immediately',0],['Waiting until the end of the shift to report a splash',1]])]],
['Other laboratory safety',[
'Phlebotomists also follow **chemical**, **electrical**, **fire** and **radiation** safety rules. Every chemical has a **Safety Data Sheet (SDS)**, and labels follow the Globally Harmonized System with pictograms. For fire, remember **RACE** (Rescue, Alarm, Contain, Extinguish or Evacuate) and **PASS** for extinguishers (Pull, Aim, Squeeze, Sweep). Report frayed cords and do not use damaged equipment.',
'**Latex allergy** can cause anything from a rash to anaphylaxis; ask about allergies and use non-latex gloves and tourniquets. Clean blood spills by wearing gloves, absorbing the spill, disinfecting with an approved product and disposing of waste in **biohazard** bags. Eating, drinking, applying cosmetics or handling contact lenses in the lab is prohibited.',
"Personal safety also includes **ergonomics** and **patient safety**. Raise or lower beds to a working height, avoid twisting while drawing, and lock bed wheels. Before leaving an inpatient room, return the bed and **side rails** to their original position, place the **call light** within reach and remove all supplies—a forgotten needle cap, alcohol pad wrapper or tourniquet left on a bed can cause injury or infection. Never leave a tourniquet on a patient’s arm.",
flip('Safety acronyms and rules',[['RACE','Rescue, Alarm, Contain, Extinguish/Evacuate'],['PASS','Pull, Aim, Squeeze, Sweep'],['SDS','Safety Data Sheet with hazards and first aid'],['Sharps container','Replace at about ¾ full'],['Hepatitis B vaccine','Offered free by employers to at-risk staff'],['Spill cleanup','Gloves, absorb, disinfect, biohazard waste']])]],
['Practice scenarios',[
kase('Exposure scenario',{a:{text:'After a draw, you feel a prick through your glove as you activate the safety device. What do you do first?',choices:[['Wash the site with soap and water','b','Correct. Immediate washing is the first step.',true],['Squeeze the site hard and keep working','b','Wash it, then report.',false]]},b:{text:'What comes next?',choices:[['Report it to your supervisor immediately and seek medical evaluation','c','Correct. Prompt evaluation allows timely post-exposure treatment.',true],['Tell no one if it does not bleed','c','All exposures must be reported.',false]]},c:{text:'You document the incident. The employer’s exposure plan guides testing and follow-up.',end:true}}),
chk('Which washing time should you verbalize if the scenario says it is the start of your workday?',['2 minutes','20 seconds','1–2 minutes','10 seconds'],'Start of the workday = 2 minutes.')]]
],takeaways:['Standard precautions treat all blood as infectious; break the chain of infection; add contact, droplet or airborne precautions as needed.','Skill I: jewelry off, warm water, fingertips down, friction, interlace, nails, wrists; verbalize 2 minutes (start of day), 20 seconds (between patients) or 1–2 minutes (soiled); rinse hands down; pat dry; towel off.','Skill VI: pull the first glove inside out and hold it, bare fingers under the second wrist, turn it over the first; never recap; activate safety devices; wash, report and seek care after exposures.'],terms:[['Standard precautions','Treating all blood and body fluids as potentially infectious.'],['Engineering control','Equipment such as safety needles that removes or isolates a hazard.'],['Post-exposure prophylaxis','Medicine given after an exposure to prevent infection.']],mcqs:[q('How long should you wash hands at the start of the workday in Skill I?','2 minutes','20 seconds','5 minutes','1 minute','Between patients is 20 seconds.'),q('During rinsing, hands should be:','Pointed down and lower than the elbows','Pointed up','Level with the shoulders','Held under the soap dispenser','Water runs off the fingertips.'),q('How do you dry your hands in Skill I?','Pat with a clean paper towel','Shake them dry','Wipe on scrubs','Use an air dryer only','Patting protects skin.'),q('In Skill VI, where does the first glove end up?','Inside the second glove','In your pocket','On the counter','On the floor','No exterior surfaces exposed.'),q('Which law requires safety-engineered sharps?','Needlestick Safety and Prevention Act','HIPAA','CLIA','EMTALA','Passed in 2000.'),q('When should a sharps container be replaced?','When about three-quarters full','When overflowing','Once a year','Never','Avoid overfilling.'),q('After a needlestick, the first step is to:','Wash the site with soap and water','Finish your shift','Apply a bandage only','Call the patient’s family','Then report immediately.'),q('What does the P in PASS stand for?','Pull the pin','Point','Push','Pause','Pull, Aim, Squeeze, Sweep.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you would wash your hands and remove contaminated gloves following HOSA Skills I and VI, and describe what to do if you receive a needlestick.',rubric:[['Handwashing technique',['jewelry','warm','fingertips down','lather','friction','interlace','nails','wrists']],['Washing times and finish',['2 minutes','20 seconds','1-2 minutes','lower than the elbows','pat','paper towel','faucet']],['Glove removal',['outside','below the wrist','inside out','hold','fingers under','inside the second']],['Needlestick response',['wash','soap','report','supervisor','medical','document','prophylaxis']]],model:'I remove all jewelry, turn on warm water and wet my hands with fingertips pointing down, never letting my clothes or hands touch the sink. I lather with a circular motion and friction, interlace my fingers, scratch the nails of each hand against the opposite palm and wash my wrists. I verbalize the time: 2 minutes at the start of the workday, 20 seconds between patients and procedures or 1–2 minutes if my hands are soiled. I rinse with my hands pointed down and lower than my elbows, pat my hands and wrists dry with a clean paper towel, discard it without touching the receptacle and use a clean, dry towel to turn off the faucet. To remove gloves, I grasp the outside of one glove below the wrist with my other gloved hand, pull it inside out and off and hold it in the gloved hand; then I slip the fingers of my bare hand under the wrist of the remaining glove and pull it inside out over the first glove so no outside surfaces are exposed, discard them and perform hand hygiene. If I receive a needlestick, I immediately wash the site with soap and water, report it to my supervisor, document it and get prompt medical evaluation so post-exposure prophylaxis can be started if needed.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_HH,OSHA_BBP,CDC_SHARPS]};

export const H3={title:'Blood, the circulatory system and vein selection',hook:'A single tube of blood holds billions of cells and hundreds of chemicals that reveal how the body is working. To collect it safely you need to know what blood is made of, how it clots and exactly where the veins—and the nerves and arteries hiding near them—run in the arm. The HOSA sample question asks which antecubital vein is the last choice, and why.',objectives:['Describe the components of blood and the difference between serum and plasma.','Identify the types of white blood cells and their functions.','Explain hemostasis and coagulation.','Select antecubital veins in the correct order of preference and avoid nerves and arteries.'],sections:[
['The circulatory system',[
'The **heart** pumps blood through two circuits. The **pulmonary circuit** carries blood from the right ventricle to the lungs to pick up oxygen, and the **systemic circuit** carries oxygen-rich blood from the left ventricle to the body. **Arteries** carry blood away from the heart under high pressure; their walls are thick and they **pulsate**. **Veins** carry blood toward the heart under low pressure; they have thinner walls and **valves** that prevent backflow. **Capillaries** connect them and are where oxygen, nutrients and wastes are exchanged.',
'Venipuncture uses **veins** because they lie closer to the surface, have lower pressure and are easier to access. Arterial blood is collected only by specially trained staff for **blood gases**. Capillary blood, a mixture of arterial and venous blood with tissue fluid, is collected by skin puncture.',
label('heart','The heart and great vessels',[['ra','Right atrium','Receives deoxygenated blood from the body.'],['rv','Right ventricle','Pumps blood to the lungs.'],['la','Left atrium','Receives oxygenated blood from the lungs.'],['lv','Left ventricle','Pumps blood to the body; thickest wall.'],['aorta','Aorta','Largest artery.'],['svc','Superior vena cava','Returns blood from the upper body, including the arms.']])]],
['What blood is made of',[
'Blood is about **55% plasma** and **45% formed elements**. **Plasma** is the liquid portion—about 91% water with proteins (albumin, globulins, **fibrinogen**), glucose, electrolytes, hormones and wastes. **Formed elements** are **red blood cells (erythrocytes)**, which carry oxygen using hemoglobin and live about **120 days**; **white blood cells (leukocytes)**, which fight infection; and **platelets (thrombocytes)**, cell fragments that help blood clot. All are made in the **red bone marrow**.',
'**Plasma** is obtained from an **anticoagulated** tube that is centrifuged; it **contains fibrinogen** and other clotting factors. **Serum** is the fluid left after blood **clots** in a tube without anticoagulant; it **lacks fibrinogen** because fibrinogen was used up to form the clot. Many chemistry tests use serum; coagulation tests use plasma; a complete blood count uses **whole blood**.',
sort('Serum, plasma or whole blood?',['Serum (clotted tube)','Plasma (anticoagulant, centrifuged)','Whole blood (anticoagulant, not centrifuged)'],[['Fluid from a red-top tube after clotting',0],['Fluid from a light-blue tube after centrifuging',1],['Lavender tube for a CBC',2],['Contains fibrinogen',1],['Lacks fibrinogen',0],['Gold SST after spinning',0]])]],
['White blood cells',[
'There are five types of leukocytes. **Granulocytes** have granules in the cytoplasm: **neutrophils** (the most numerous, about 50–70%, first responders that engulf bacteria), **eosinophils** (about 1–4%, fight parasites and take part in allergic reactions) and **basophils** (less than 1%, the **least numerous**, which release **histamine** and **heparin**). **Agranulocytes** are **lymphocytes** (about 20–40%; B cells make antibodies, T cells attack infected cells) and **monocytes** (about 2–8%, which become macrophages in tissues).',
'A complete blood count (CBC) with a **differential** reports these percentages. A high neutrophil count often suggests a bacterial infection, high lymphocytes a viral infection and high eosinophils allergies or parasites. A memory aid for the order from most to least numerous is “**Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas**.”',
match('Match the white blood cell',[['Neutrophil','Most numerous; engulfs bacteria'],['Lymphocyte','B and T cells; antibodies'],['Monocyte','Becomes a macrophage'],['Eosinophil','Parasites and allergies'],['Basophil','Least numerous; releases histamine and heparin'],['Platelet','Cell fragment that helps clotting']])]],
['Hemostasis and coagulation',[
'**Hemostasis** stops bleeding in stages. **1. Vascular spasm**: the injured vessel constricts. **2. Platelet plug**: platelets stick to the injury and to each other. **3. Coagulation**: a cascade of clotting factors converts **prothrombin to thrombin**, and thrombin converts **fibrinogen to fibrin**, which forms a mesh that strengthens the plug. **4. Fibrinolysis**: once healing is underway, plasmin dissolves the clot.',
'Coagulation tests such as **PT/INR** (monitors warfarin) and **aPTT** (monitors heparin) are drawn in **light-blue sodium citrate** tubes, which bind calcium to prevent clotting. Patients taking anticoagulants or with bleeding disorders need **longer pressure** after the draw. Petechiae (tiny red spots) below the tourniquet can signal a platelet or clotting problem.',
order('Hemostasis in order',['Vascular spasm','Platelet plug formation','Coagulation: fibrinogen becomes fibrin','Fibrinolysis dissolves the clot'])]],
['Choosing a vein',[
'The **antecubital fossa** (front of the elbow) is the first-choice area. In the common **H-shaped pattern**, the veins are the **median cubital** (first choice—large, well anchored and least painful), the **cephalic** (second choice—on the thumb side; harder to feel but safe in obese patients) and the **basilic** (last choice—on the pinky side, near the **median nerve** and the **brachial artery**, and it rolls easily). In the **M-shaped pattern**, the **median** vein is first, then the median cephalic, with the **median basilic** last for the same reason.',
'Locate a vein by **palpating** with the tip of the **index finger**—a vein feels **bouncy** and resilient; an artery **pulsates**; a tendon feels hard and cord-like. Never use the thumb, which has its own pulse. Note the vein’s position relative to skin creases, moles or scars. If antecubital veins are not usable, veins on the **back of the hand** may be used (often with a butterfly). Avoid wrist veins on the palm side and leg or foot veins unless a provider approves.',
{t:'table',caption:'Antecubital veins (H pattern)',head:['Vein','Location','Preference','Reason'],rows:[['Median cubital','Center of the antecubital fossa','First','Large, anchored, least painful'],['Cephalic','Thumb (lateral) side','Second','Away from nerves; harder to palpate'],['Basilic','Pinky (medial) side','Last','Near the median nerve and brachial artery; rolls']]},
sort('Feel test: what is it?',['Vein','Artery','Tendon'],[['Bouncy and resilient',0],['Pulsates',1],['Hard and cord-like',2],['No pulse, refills when pressed',0],['Throbbing under the fingertip',1]])]],
['Sites to avoid',[
'Do not draw from an arm on the side of a **mastectomy** (lymphedema risk) without provider permission, from an arm with an **IV infusing** (draw from the other arm or below the IV after it is stopped per policy), from a **fistula or graft** for dialysis, or from areas with **hematomas**, **burns**, **scars**, **tattoos** (if possible), **edema** or **infection**. **Sclerosed** (hardened) or **thrombosed** veins feel cord-like and roll; choose another site.',
kase('Site selection',{a:{text:'Your patient had a left mastectomy and has an IV running in the right forearm. The requisition is for routine labs. What is the best choice?',choices:[['Draw from the right arm below the IV after the nurse stops the infusion for at least 2 minutes, per policy, or notify the nurse','b','Correct. Never draw above an IV, and avoid the mastectomy side without permission.',true],['Draw from the left arm—it has no IV','b','The mastectomy side requires provider permission.',false]]},b:{text:'In the right antecubital area you feel the median cubital and a pulsing structure near the basilic vein. Which do you choose?',choices:[['The median cubital vein','c','Correct. The pulsing structure is the brachial artery—avoid it.',true],['The pulsing structure because it is easy to feel','c','A pulse means an artery.',false]]},c:{text:'You note the vein’s location relative to a skin crease and prepare to clean the site.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Arteries carry blood away from the heart and pulsate; veins carry blood back, have valves and are used for venipuncture.','Blood is 55% plasma (with fibrinogen) and 45% cells; serum is plasma without fibrinogen; neutrophils are most numerous and basophils (histamine, heparin) least.','Choose the median cubital first, cephalic second and basilic last (near the median nerve and brachial artery); palpate with the index finger; avoid mastectomy sides, IVs, fistulas, hematomas and scars.'],terms:[['Antecubital fossa','The front of the elbow where most venipunctures are done.'],['Serum','The liquid part of clotted blood; lacks fibrinogen.'],['Hemostasis','The process that stops bleeding.']],mcqs:[q('Which vein would be last choice for venipuncture because it is close to the median nerve and the brachial artery?','Median basilic vein','Median cubital vein','Median vein','Median cephalic vein','HOSA sample question (M pattern).'),q('Which white blood cells release histamine and heparin and are normally the least numerous?','Basophils','Lymphocytes','Eosinophils','Neutrophils','HOSA sample question.'),q('Which is the first-choice vein in the H pattern?','Median cubital','Basilic','Cephalic','Radial','Large and anchored.'),q('What does serum lack that plasma contains?','Fibrinogen','Water','Glucose','Electrolytes','Used up in clotting.'),q('About how long do red blood cells live?','120 days','12 hours','7 days','5 years','Removed by the spleen and liver.'),q('Which finger should be used to palpate a vein?','Index finger','Thumb','Pinky','Any finger','The thumb has a pulse.'),q('Thrombin converts fibrinogen into:','Fibrin','Plasmin','Prothrombin','Platelets','Forms the clot mesh.'),q('Which arm should be avoided without provider permission?','The arm on the side of a mastectomy','The dominant arm','The left arm always','An arm with a tattoo','Lymphedema risk.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you would choose a venipuncture site in the antecubital area, including the order of vein preference, how to tell a vein from an artery and tendon, and three conditions that would make you avoid a site.',rubric:[['Vein order',['median cubital','first','cephalic','second','basilic','last']],['Reasons',['median nerve','brachial artery','anchored','rolls']],['Palpation',['index finger','bouncy','pulsate','artery','tendon','thumb']],['Sites to avoid',['mastectomy','iv','fistula','hematoma','scar','burn','edema','sclerosed']]],model:'In the common H-shaped pattern, I choose the median cubital vein first because it is large, well anchored and least painful; the cephalic vein on the thumb side is my second choice; and the basilic vein on the pinky side is my last choice because it lies close to the median nerve and the brachial artery and tends to roll. In the M pattern, the median vein is first and the median basilic is last for the same reasons. I palpate with the tip of my index finger, never my thumb, because the thumb has its own pulse. A vein feels bouncy and resilient and has no pulse; an artery pulsates, so I avoid any structure I feel throbbing; and a tendon feels hard and cord-like. I also note the vein’s position relative to skin creases, scars or moles. I avoid the arm on the side of a mastectomy without provider permission, any site above an IV, a dialysis fistula or graft, hematomas, burns, scars, edematous or infected areas and sclerosed or thrombosed veins that feel hard and cord-like.'},sources:[RULES,OSA_BLOOD,OSA_WBC,WHO_BLOOD]};

export const H4={title:'Equipment, tube additives, the order of draw and the tourniquet (Skill II)',hook:'Every color of stopper means a different additive, and every additive suits different tests. Put the tubes in the wrong order and an anticoagulant from one tube can contaminate the next, giving a falsely high potassium or a falsely abnormal clotting result. Obtaining blood samples—including which tubes are used for which tests—is 42% of the test.',objectives:['Identify the parts of the evacuated tube system (ETS), needles and other equipment.','Match tube colors to their additives, tests and inversions.','State the order of draw and explain why it matters.','Apply and release a tourniquet following HOSA Skill II.'],sections:[
['The evacuated tube system',[
'The **evacuated tube system (ETS)** has three parts: a **multisample needle**, a **tube holder** (adapter) and **evacuated tubes** with a premeasured **vacuum** that pulls in the right amount of blood. The multisample needle has a rubber **sleeve** over the back end so tubes can be changed without leaking. Holders are **single-use**—they are discarded with the needle. **Needle gauge** describes the diameter: the **higher the gauge number, the smaller the needle**. Routine venipuncture uses **21- or 22-gauge** needles; smaller (23-gauge) butterflies help with small or fragile veins.',
'Other supplies include **tourniquets** (usually latex-free), **70% isopropyl alcohol** pads, **gauze** (not cotton balls, which stick to the clot), **bandages**, **gloves**, a **sharps container**, labels or a printer, and specimen bags. **Syringes** are sometimes used for very fragile veins, with a **transfer device** to fill tubes safely.',
match('Equipment',[['Multisample needle','Double-ended needle that pierces vein and tube'],['Tube holder','Single-use adapter that holds the needle'],['21 gauge','Standard routine venipuncture size'],['23-gauge butterfly','Small or fragile veins, hands'],['Gauze pad','Applies pressure without sticking to the clot'],['Transfer device','Safely moves blood from a syringe into tubes']])]],
['Tube colors and additives',[
'**Light blue** contains **sodium citrate** and is used for **coagulation** tests (PT/INR, aPTT); it must be **filled completely** because the 9:1 blood-to-citrate ratio matters. **Red** (plastic contains a clot activator; glass has no additive) gives **serum** for chemistry, serology and blood bank. **Gold** or **red/gray mottled (SST)** has clot activator and **gel** that separates serum during centrifuging. **Green** contains **heparin** (lithium or sodium) for **plasma chemistry** and STAT tests; **light green/green-gray (PST)** adds gel.',
'**Lavender/purple** contains **EDTA** for **hematology**—CBC, hemoglobin A1c, ESR. **Pink** EDTA is used in the **blood bank** (type and crossmatch), and **white/pearl** EDTA with gel for molecular testing. **Gray** contains **sodium fluoride and potassium oxalate** to stop glycolysis for **glucose, lactate and blood alcohol**. **Royal blue** tubes are certified free of trace metals. **Yellow SPS** bottles or tubes are used for **blood cultures**.',
{t:'table',caption:'Common tubes (inversions follow typical manufacturer guidance)',head:['Stopper','Additive','Common tests','Inversions'],rows:[['Light blue','Sodium citrate','PT/INR, aPTT','3–4'],['Red / gold (SST)','Clot activator (± gel)','Chemistry, serology','5'],['Green / light green','Heparin (± gel)','Plasma chemistry, STAT','8–10'],['Lavender / pink','EDTA','CBC, A1c, blood bank','8–10'],['Gray','Sodium fluoride / potassium oxalate','Glucose, lactate, alcohol','8–10']]},
match('Tube to test',[['Light blue','PT/INR'],['Lavender','CBC'],['Gray','Glucose tolerance test'],['Gold SST','Comprehensive metabolic panel'],['Green','STAT plasma potassium'],['Pink','Type and crossmatch'],['Yellow SPS','Blood culture'],['Royal blue','Trace elements such as zinc or lead']])]],
['The order of draw',[
'The **CLSI** order of draw is: **1.** blood cultures (sterile) → **2.** **light blue** (citrate) → **3.** **red / gold** (serum, with or without clot activator or gel) → **4.** **green** (heparin) → **5.** **lavender / pink / pearl** (EDTA) → **6.** **gray** (fluoride/oxalate). HOSA’s rating sheet lists the four groups used in the scenario: **light blue; red, red/gray mottled or gold; light or dark green, green/gray mottled; lavender/purple, pink or white/pearl**.',
'The order prevents **additive carryover**. EDTA contains potassium and binds calcium, so if it contaminates a chemistry tube, it causes **falsely high potassium and falsely low calcium**. Clot activator in a light-blue tube would distort clotting results. A popular memory aid is “**B**ecause **L**ab **R**esults **G**et **L**ost **G**oing” (blood cultures, light blue, red/gold, green, lavender, gray). Gently invert each tube as soon as it is removed—**never shake**, which causes hemolysis.',
order('Put the tubes in CLSI order',['Blood culture bottles','Light blue (sodium citrate)','Red or gold (serum)','Green (heparin)','Lavender (EDTA)','Gray (fluoride/oxalate)']),
chk('Why must EDTA be drawn after the serum and heparin tubes?',['EDTA carryover falsely raises potassium and lowers calcium','EDTA makes the tubes heavier','EDTA clots the blood','It does not matter'],'K₂EDTA contains potassium and chelates calcium.')]],
['The tourniquet: Skill II',[
'A tourniquet slows venous return so veins fill and are easier to find, without stopping arterial flow. Skill II has 10 steps in 2 minutes. **1.** Place the tourniquet **3–4 inches above** the intended site. **2.** Grasp one side in each hand a few inches from the ends. **3.** Apply a small amount of **tension** and keep it. **4.** Bring the two sides together and grasp both between the thumb and forefinger of the right hand. **5.** Reach over the right hand, grasp the right side with the left thumb and forefinger and release it from the right hand.',
'**6.** Cross the left end over the right end near the left index finger and grasp both sides together close to the arm. **7.** Use the left middle finger or right index finger to **tuck** a portion of the left side under the right side, pulling it into a **loop**. **8.** The **ends point toward the shoulder** so they do not contaminate the site. **9.** Verbalize that the tourniquet stays on **no longer than one minute**. **10.** Remove it by pulling the loose end.',
order('Tie the tourniquet',['Place 3–4 inches above the site','Grasp each side and apply tension','Bring sides together in the right hand','Transfer the right side to the left hand','Cross left over right near the arm','Tuck a portion under to form a loop','Ends point toward the shoulder','Verbalize no more than 1 minute; release'])]],
['Why the one-minute limit matters',[
'Leaving a tourniquet on longer than **one minute** causes **hemoconcentration**: fluid leaves the blood, so cells, proteins and some analytes (potassium, protein, cholesterol) become falsely concentrated. Prolonged tourniquet time and vigorous **fist pumping** can falsely raise potassium. If it takes longer than a minute to find a vein, **release the tourniquet**, wait about **two minutes** and reapply.',
'A tourniquet that is too tight can stop arterial flow, cause **petechiae** or bruising and hurt the patient; one that is rolled or twisted is uncomfortable and less effective. The rating sheet in Skill III asks for a tourniquet that feels **slightly tight but not rolled or twisted**. Use a blood pressure cuff inflated below the diastolic pressure (often about 40 mm Hg) for difficult veins only if trained.',
sort('Effect on the specimen',['Causes hemoconcentration or false results','Good practice'],[['Tourniquet on 3 minutes',0],['Vigorous fist pumping',0],['Releasing within 1 minute',1],['Releasing and waiting 2 minutes before reapplying',1],['Ends pointing toward the shoulder',1]])]],
['Practice: plan the draw',[
{t:'example',title:'Plan the tubes',prompt:'The requisition lists a PT/INR, a comprehensive metabolic panel (SST), a STAT ammonia (green, on ice) and a CBC. In what order do you draw? (Invented for practice.)',steps:['Light blue for PT/INR','Gold SST for the metabolic panel','Green for ammonia (place on ice immediately)','Lavender for CBC'],answer:'Light blue → gold → green → lavender.'},
kase('Order of draw check',{a:{text:'A trainee plans lavender, then light blue, then gold. What is wrong?',choices:[['Lavender (EDTA) must come after light blue and gold to prevent carryover','b','Correct. EDTA contaminates coagulation and chemistry tubes.',true],['Nothing; any order works','b','Order prevents additive carryover.',false]]},b:{text:'The trainee shakes the lavender tube hard to mix it. What should they do instead?',choices:[['Invert gently 8–10 times','c','Correct. Shaking causes hemolysis.',true],['Shake it longer','c','Never shake blood tubes.',false]]},c:{text:'The trainee redraws with the correct order and gentle inversions.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['The ETS uses a multisample needle, single-use holder and evacuated tubes; higher gauge means a smaller needle; 21–22 G is routine.','Light blue citrate (coag, fill completely), red/gold serum, green heparin, lavender/pink EDTA (CBC, blood bank), gray fluoride (glucose); invert gently, never shake.','Order: cultures, light blue, red/gold, green, lavender, gray; tourniquet 3–4 inches above, ends toward the shoulder, no more than 1 minute.'],terms:[['Order of draw','The sequence for filling tubes to prevent additive carryover.'],['Hemoconcentration','A falsely increased concentration of blood components from prolonged tourniquet time.'],['Gauge','A needle’s diameter; higher numbers are smaller.']],mcqs:[q('Which tube is used for PT/INR?','Light blue','Lavender','Gray','Green','Sodium citrate.'),q('Which tube is used for a CBC?','Lavender','Light blue','Gold','Gray','EDTA preserves cells.'),q('Which tube is drawn first among light blue, green, lavender and gold?','Light blue','Green','Lavender','Gold','Then gold, green, lavender.'),q('How far above the site is the tourniquet placed?','3–4 inches','1 inch','8–10 inches','At the wrist','Per Skill II.'),q('What is the maximum tourniquet time?','1 minute','3 minutes','5 minutes','30 seconds is required','Prevents hemoconcentration.'),q('Gray-top tubes are used for glucose because they contain:','Sodium fluoride, which stops glycolysis','EDTA','Heparin','Clot activator','Cells keep using glucose otherwise.'),q('EDTA carryover into a chemistry tube causes:','Falsely high potassium','Falsely high calcium','No change','Falsely low potassium','K₂EDTA contains potassium.'),q('Which needle is smallest in diameter?','25 gauge','18 gauge','21 gauge','16 gauge','Higher number, smaller needle.')],frq:{prompt:'A requisition lists a CBC, an aPTT, a basic metabolic panel in an SST and a fasting glucose in a gray-top tube. List the tubes in the correct order of draw, name each additive and explain why the order and the tourniquet time both matter.',rubric:[['Correct order',['light blue','gold','lavender','gray']],['Additives',['sodium citrate','clot activator','gel','edta','sodium fluoride','oxalate']],['Why order matters',['carryover','potassium','calcium','contaminate','false']],['Tourniquet',['1 minute','hemoconcentration','3-4 inches','release','2 minutes']]],model:'I draw the light-blue sodium citrate tube first for the aPTT and make sure it fills completely to keep the 9:1 ratio; then the gold SST, which contains clot activator and gel, for the basic metabolic panel; then the lavender EDTA tube for the CBC; and last the gray tube, which contains sodium fluoride and potassium oxalate, for the glucose. The order of draw prevents additive carryover from one tube to the next: for example, EDTA contains potassium and binds calcium, so EDTA contamination of the SST would cause falsely high potassium and falsely low calcium, and clot activator in a coagulation tube would distort the aPTT. I invert each tube gently the recommended number of times and never shake it. The tourniquet goes 3–4 inches above the site and must stay on no longer than one minute, because longer times cause hemoconcentration and falsely raise analytes such as potassium and protein; if I need more time to find a vein, I release it, wait about two minutes and reapply it.'},sources:[RULES,WHO_BLOOD,MEDLINE_VENI,AMT]};

export const H5={title:'Routine ETS venipuncture (Skill III-A)',hook:'A routine venipuncture takes only a few minutes, but Skill III-A has 41 scored steps, and a single miss—forgetting to release the tourniquet before removing the needle, or labeling tubes away from the patient—can injure a patient or ruin a specimen. This lesson walks through the entire procedure in order, with the reasons behind each step.',objectives:['Prepare the patient, site and equipment for an ETS venipuncture.','Insert the needle at the correct angle and fill tubes in the order of draw.','Remove the needle safely, care for the site and label specimens correctly.','Recognize common errors that cause hemolysis, hematomas or wrong-patient specimens.'],sections:[
['Preparing the patient: steps 1–8',[
'**1.** Review the requisition. **2.** Approach, greet the patient and identify yourself. **3.** Ask the patient to **state their full name and date of birth and spell their first and last names**. **4.** Verbally **match** the identification to the requisition. **5.** Explain the procedure and **obtain consent**. **6.** **Verify collection requirements** (fasting, timing, special handling) and identify **sensitivities and potential problems**—latex allergy, a history of fainting, a mastectomy or anticoagulant use. **7.** Perform hand hygiene and put on gloves.',
'**8.** **Position the arm**: the patient sits in a phlebotomy chair with an armrest (never on a stool or standing) or lies down, with the arm extended downward in a straight line from shoulder to wrist and supported. A downward slope helps prevent **reflux** of additives into the vein. Remove gum or food from the patient’s mouth to prevent choking if they faint.',
order('Steps 1–8',['Review the requisition','Greet the patient and identify yourself','Patient states and spells name and gives DOB','Match identification to the requisition','Explain and obtain consent','Verify requirements and sensitivities','Hand hygiene and gloves','Position the arm downward and supported'])]],
['Finding and cleaning the site: steps 9–15',[
'**9.** Apply the tourniquet **3–4 inches above** the site, slightly tight but not rolled or twisted. **10.** Ask the patient to **clench a fist** (no pumping). **11.** **Palpate** the antecubital veins with the tip of your index finger and select one. **12.** **Release the tourniquet** and ask the patient to open the fist—this keeps the one-minute limit while you prepare. **13.** Note the vein’s position relative to hair, skin creases, scars or a mole.',
'**14.** Clean the site with **70% isopropyl alcohol** using **back-and-forth friction** with adequate pressure (current CLSI practice; some references teach concentric circles). **15.** Let the site **air-dry** completely—do not blow on it, fan it or touch it again. Wet alcohol stings and can **hemolyze** the specimen. If you must re-palpate after cleaning, clean the site again.',
chk('Why let the alcohol dry completely?',['Wet alcohol stings and can hemolyze the specimen','It makes the vein bigger','It is not necessary','To save time'],'Air-drying also lets the alcohol work.')]],
['Inserting the needle: steps 16–24',[
'**16.** Prepare equipment: attach the **needle to the ETS holder** and have tubes in the order of draw. **17.** **Reapply the tourniquet**. **18.** **Uncap and inspect** the needle for burrs or defects. **19.** Ask the patient to remake a fist. **20.** **Anchor** the vein by drawing the skin taut with your thumb **1–2 inches below** the site. **21.** **Warn** the patient (“You will feel a poke”). **22.** Line the needle up with the vein and insert it **bevel up** in one smooth forward motion at a **15–30 degree angle**.',
'**23.** Stop advancing when you feel a **decrease in resistance** (a slight “give”), and ask the patient to **release the fist**. **24.** Grasp the **short wings (flanges) of the tube holder** with your index and middle fingers. Keeping the holder steady prevents the needle from moving in the vein, which can cause pain, a hematoma or nerve injury.',
order('Steps 16–24',['Attach needle to holder; tubes ready in order','Reapply tourniquet','Uncap and inspect the needle','Patient makes a fist','Anchor the skin 1–2 inches below the site','Warn the patient','Insert bevel up at 15–30° in one smooth motion','Stop at the decrease in resistance; patient releases fist','Grasp the holder flanges with index and middle fingers'])]],
['Filling the tubes: steps 25–29',[
'**25.** Push the tube onto the back of the needle with your **thumb**. **26.** Keep the device from moving throughout. **27.** Let the tube fill until the **vacuum is exhausted** and blood flow stops—underfilled tubes give wrong ratios of blood to additive. **28.** Fill tubes in the **order of draw** (the scenario uses all four groups: light blue; red/gold; green; lavender/pink/pearl), removing each and **gently inverting** it as you go.',
'**29.** **Remove the tourniquet when the last tube starts to fill** (or within one minute). Releasing it before removing the needle prevents blood from being forced out of the puncture and reduces hematomas. If blood does not flow, the needle may be too shallow, too deep, against the vein wall or beside the vein; adjust slightly or use a new tube if the vacuum is lost—never probe blindly. After two unsuccessful attempts, ask another phlebotomist to try.',
{t:'w',kind:'sort',title:'Fill in order',buckets:['Fill 1st','Fill 2nd','Fill 3rd','Fill 4th'],items:[['Light blue',0],['Gold SST',1],['Red',1],['Green',2],['Light green PST',2],['Lavender',3],['Pink',3]]}]],
['Finishing the draw: steps 30–41',[
'**30.** Remove the needle in a **smooth motion** and **activate the safety feature** immediately. **31.** Place a clean folded **gauze pad** over the site with your free hand and apply pressure; mix tubes per manufacturer instructions. **32.** Ask the patient to keep pressure on the site until bleeding stops (arm **straight**, not bent—bending can cause a hematoma). **33.** Check the arm for bleeding and apply a **bandage**. **34.** Discard the needle and holder together in the **sharps container**.',
'**35.** **Label** tubes at the patient’s side, in the patient’s presence, with at least the patient’s **full name, date of birth or ID number, date and time of collection and your initials**, and prepare them for transport in a specimen bag. **36.** Tell the patient to keep pressure for a minimum of **15 minutes**—per the rating sheet—and to avoid exertion with that arm. **37.** Dispose of materials and clean the area. **38.** Thank the patient. **39.** Remove gloves and perform hand hygiene. **40–41.** Communicate appropriately and use standard precautions throughout.',
flip('Finishing details',[['Needle out','Smooth motion; activate safety device'],['Pressure','Folded gauze; arm straight'],['Bandage','Only after bleeding stops'],['Sharps','Needle and holder together'],['Label','At bedside: name, DOB/ID, date, time, initials'],['Patient instructions','Pressure, avoid exertion; thank the patient']])]],
['Errors and specimen quality',[
'**Hemolysis** (ruptured red cells) falsely raises potassium and other analytes and can make specimens unusable. Causes include using too small a needle with a large vacuum tube, drawing through wet alcohol, **shaking** tubes, pulling a syringe plunger too hard and probing. **Hematomas** form when blood leaks into tissue—from the needle going through the vein, too little pressure, bending the arm or removing the needle before the tourniquet. Wrong-patient and mislabeled specimens are prevented only by strict identification and bedside labeling.',
kase('Troubleshoot the draw',{a:{text:'You insert the needle, push in the first tube and no blood flows. A slight bulge appears above the needle tip. What is likely happening?',choices:[['The needle may be partly through or beside the vein; slowly withdraw slightly to reposition','b','Correct. Small adjustments can restore flow; never probe.',true],['The tube is defective—keep pushing deeper','b','Pushing deeper risks going through the vein and hitting a nerve.',false]]},b:{text:'Flow returns, but a lump starts to form around the site. What do you do?',choices:[['Release the tourniquet, remove the needle, apply pressure and choose another site if needed','c','Correct. A forming hematoma means stop.',true],['Continue filling all the tubes','c','Continuing worsens the hematoma.',false]]},c:{text:'After pressure, you redraw from the other arm with a new needle and fresh tubes. Specimen quality protected.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Identify, explain and consent, verify requirements and sensitivities, glove, position the arm down and supported; tourniquet 3–4 inches above, palpate, release, clean with friction and air-dry.','Reapply the tourniquet, inspect the needle, anchor 1–2 inches below, warn, insert bevel up at 15–30°, hold the holder steady and fill tubes in the order of draw until the vacuum is exhausted.','Remove the tourniquet as the last tube fills, withdraw and activate the safety device, gauze pressure with the arm straight, bandage, sharps, label at bedside and thank the patient.'],terms:[['Bevel','The slanted tip of the needle, inserted facing up.'],['Hemolysis','Rupture of red blood cells, which can ruin a specimen.'],['Hematoma','A collection of blood under the skin from leakage at the puncture site.']],mcqs:[q('At what angle is the needle inserted for an ETS venipuncture?','15–30 degrees','45–60 degrees','90 degrees','5 degrees','Bevel up.'),q('Where do you anchor the vein?','1–2 inches below the site with the thumb','Above the site with the index finger','On the opposite arm','Over the needle','Draw the skin taut.'),q('When is the tourniquet removed?','When the last tube starts to fill','After the needle is removed','Before inserting the needle','After bandaging','Prevents hematomas.'),q('What should you clean the site with?','70% isopropyl alcohol','Iodine for every draw','Soap only','Hydrogen peroxide','Iodine is for blood cultures.'),q('Why should the patient keep the arm straight after the draw?','Bending can cause a hematoma','It helps them relax','It speeds the test','It is not important','Apply pressure with the arm straight.'),q('When should tubes be labeled?','At the bedside in the patient’s presence','Back in the lab','Before meeting the patient','The next day','Prevents mislabeling.'),q('Which can cause hemolysis?','Shaking the tubes','Gentle inversion','Letting alcohol dry','Using a 21-gauge needle','Shaking ruptures red cells.'),q('Per the rating sheet, how long should the patient maintain pressure at minimum?','15 minutes','1 minute','5 seconds','1 hour','And avoid exertion with that arm.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the steps of a routine ETS venipuncture from inserting the needle through labeling the tubes, and explain how each step protects the patient or the specimen.',rubric:[['Insertion',['reapply','inspect','anchor','1-2 inches','warn','bevel up','15-30']],['Collection',['decrease in resistance','release fist','holder','vacuum','order of draw','invert']],['Removal and site care',['tourniquet','last tube','smooth','safety','gauze','pressure','straight','bandage']],['Labeling and closing',['sharps','label','bedside','name','date','time','initials','thank']]],model:'I reapply the tourniquet, uncap and inspect the needle for defects, have the patient make a fist, anchor the vein by pulling the skin taut 1–2 inches below the site and warn the patient. I insert the needle bevel up at a 15–30 degree angle in one smooth motion, which minimizes pain and the risk of going through the vein, and stop when I feel a decrease in resistance, asking the patient to release the fist. I hold the holder flanges steady so the needle does not move, push each tube onto the needle with my thumb and let it fill until the vacuum is exhausted so the blood-to-additive ratio is correct, filling in the order of draw—light blue, red or gold, green, lavender—to prevent additive carryover, and gently inverting each tube. I remove the tourniquet as the last tube starts to fill so blood is not forced out of the puncture, withdraw the needle smoothly and activate the safety device to prevent needlesticks, then apply a folded gauze pad with the arm straight to prevent a hematoma and bandage it once bleeding stops. I discard the needle and holder in the sharps container, label the tubes at the bedside with the patient’s name, date of birth or ID, the date and time and my initials to prevent identification errors, bag them for transport, give aftercare instructions and thank the patient.'},sources:[RULES,WHO_BLOOD,MEDLINE_VENI,NPSG]};
